 . When a user requests resources, the cloud provider creates a virtual machine (VM) for them.
 . The VM is managed using virtualization, which provides the illusion of dedicated hardware — the user feels like they have their own CPU, RAM, and storage.
 . In reality, many VMs share the same physical hardware (CPUs, memory, storage), but the hypervisor ensures isolation: one VM cannot directly access or interfere with another.
 . This allows efficient utilization of resources while still giving each user a separate, secure environment.

