//Hardware level virtualisation 
A technique that creates a virtual version of a physical computer.
On this virtual computer, you can run a guest operating system just like it’s on a real machine.

    //how it works 
     1. hypervisor sits between guest os nad hardware 
     2. It emulates the physical hardware so the guest OS thinks it’s running on a real machine.
     3. It allocates CPU, memory, storage, and I/O devices to each VM safely.
  
   it is called as system virtualisation 
    Because it virtualizes the whole system hardware (CPU, memory, devices) for the guest OS.
    It provides the ISA (instruction set interface) to the VM, so programs inside the guest can run as if on real hardware.

