//hypervisor 
it si a software that mediates between the vms and the underlying hardware 
also called VMM (virtual machine manager )

//Type 1 hypervisor 
    Runs directly on physical hardware — no host OS required.
    Manages hardware and multiple VMs directly.
    Often used in data centers and enterprise servers.
key points : 
 High performance, low overhead.
 Strong isolation between VMs.
 //flow 
 Hardware → Type-1 Hypervisor → Guest OS / VMs → Applications
 ex:vmware ESXi
 
 //type 2 hypervisor 
 Runs on top of a host OS, just like a normal application.
 VMs run on this hypervisor.

  Easier to install and use on desktops or laptops.
  Slightly lower performance due to host OS layer.

  //flow 
  Hardware → Host OS → Type-2 Hypervisor → Guest OS / VMs → Applications
  ex: oracle virtual box 

   | Feature     | Type-1 Hypervisor         | Type-2 Hypervisor              |
| ----------- | ------------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| Runs on     | Bare metal (hardware)     | Host OS                        |
| Performance | High                      | Lower (extra host OS layer)    |
| Use case    | Servers, data centers     | Desktops, testing environments |
| Isolation   | Strong                    | Moderate                       |
| Examples    | VMware ESXi, Xen, Hyper-V | VMware Workstation, VirtualBox |

//VMM consists of 3 modules 
 1. dispatcher 
 2. allocator 
 3. interpreter 

 1.dispatcher 
   entry point of vmm 
   Receives requests from the VM and decides where to send them: either to the allocator or the interpreter.
 2. allocator 
    Decides how much physical resources (CPU, memory, I/O) to give to each VM.
    Invoked by the dispatcher when a VM needs resources.
 3. interpreter 
      Handles privileged instructions that a VM cannot execute directly on hardware.
      vm cannot execute these directly so interpreter executes these instruction on behalf of the vm 

//conditions for VMM to wrok efficiently 
 1. equivalence 
   A VM should behave exactly like a real physical machine.
   Programs inside the VM shouldn’t “know” they are virtualized — they should run as if on actual hardware.
 2. resource control 
     The hypervisor must fully control all virtualized resources: CPU, memory, storage, and I/O.
     Prevents VMs from interfering with each other or using more resources than allowed.
 3. efficiency 
     Most instructions executed by the VM should run directly on the hardware without going through the hypervisor.
      Minimizes performance overhead from virtualization.
      normal aritmetic and all those operations execute natively only previleged instruction trigger the hypervisor 


//protection ring concept 
   Protection rings are a way to control access to computer resources in a layered manner.
   They define levels of privilege for code execution in the CPU.

   //common rings 
     | Ring       | Privilege Level | Typical Use                                             |
    | ---------- | --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
    | **Ring 0** | Highest         | Operating system kernel, hypervisors, device drivers    |
    | Ring 1     | Medium          | OS services (sometimes used, not common in modern OSes) |
    | Ring 2     | Medium          | OS services / drivers (rare)                            |
    | **Ring 3** | Lowest          | User applications (browsers, word processors, games)    |

