//hardware virtualisation techniques 
1. full virtualisation 
   Full virtualization allows you to run an guest(unmodified) operating system on a virtual machine as if it were running on real hardware.
   vmms are required to provide a complete emulation fo the entire underlying hardware 
   this leads to complete isolation , more security 
   //how it works 
   1. hypervisor operates at ring0 
   2. it manages all hardware access to the guest os 
   3. guest tries to execute all previliges instructions 
   4. to guest os it feel slike talking directly to hardware 
   5.Hypervisor intercepts and translates these instructions but hypervisor secretly manages it 
   6. each vm is completely isolated 

 Full Virtualization creates completely separate virtual machines (VMs),
 each running its own full operating system — as if it were running on real, physical hardware.

//para virtualisation 
   Para-virtualization is a technique where the guest OS is slightly modified to interact with the hypervisor efficiently.
   Instead of trying to execute privileged instructions directly, the guest OS makes explicit calls (hypercalls) to the hypervisor.

  //how it works 
  1. guest os is modified so it doesnt execute previliged instruction 
  2. it executes hypercalls to the hypervisor 
  3. hypervisor executees the operation safely 

  | Aspect                  | Full Virtualization                         | Para-Virtualization                                            |
| ----------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |
| Guest OS                | Unmodified                                  | Modified                                                       |
| Privileged Instructions | Guest OS executes, hypervisor traps/handles | Guest OS **does not execute directly**, always uses hypercalls |
| Performance             | Slight overhead (trapping instructions)     | Better performance (direct hypercall)                          |
| Flexibility             | Can run unmodified OS                       | Requires OS modification                                       |

 *Paravirtualization is a type of virtualization where the guest OS is slightly modified so
 it can communicate directly with the hypervisor — instead of pretending it's running on real hardware.

//partial virtualisation 
   Partial virtualization only emulates some aspects of hardware, not everything.
   The guest OS cannot run completely in isolation, and some privileged operations might not be supported.
   ex: address sppace virtualisation used in time sharing systems 
   allows many applications to run concurrently sharing the same hardware resources 
 
 * Partial Virtualization means only some parts of the hardware (like memory or CPU) are virtualized —
   so the guest OS can run, but not every feature of the system is fully emulated or isolated.
 
